## Why This Is Asked

Tests critical authorization paths, data-loss prevention, and audit integrity in multi-tenant setups, plus resilience to flaky services and retention policies.

## Key Concepts

- RBAC and DLP enforcement across tenants
- Tamper-evident audit logs (sequencing, signatures/HMAC)
- End-to-end data export redaction
- Seeds and mocks for deterministic, isolated tests
- Flaky service handling and time-based retention checks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo end-to-end test sketch (Playwright/Cypress-agnostic)
async function testRBACAndDLP() {
  const t = seedTenant('tenantA');
  const uOwner = seedUser(t, 'owner');
  const uEditor = seedUser(t, 'editor');
  const uAuditor = seedUser(t, 'auditor');
  const admin = seedUser(t, 'admin');

  // Owner creates a share with PII
  await login(uOwner);
  await createShare({ tenant: t.id, data: 'PII' });

  // Editor attempts restricted action
  await login(uEditor);
  assertAccessDenied(editShare(t.id));

  // Auditor reads audit trail
  await login(uAuditor);
  assertAuditIncludes(t.id, 'READ', uAuditor.id);

  // Admin exports with redaction
  await login(admin);
  const exportPayload = await exportData(t.id);
  assertRedacts(exportPayload, ['PII']);
  // Verify tamper-evident log
  assertTamperEvidence(t.id);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's understanding of end-to-end testing for multi-step user journeys, including authentication flows, external system mocking, and deterministic test data management. It evaluates practical knowledge of creating reliable, fast, and isolated tests in a real-world SaaS context.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing with authentication and email verification
- Test data seeding and database isolation
- Mocking external services (email delivery, OIDC sign-in)
- Assertions for UI state and created resources
- Time-bounded test design for CI/CD pipelines

## Code Example

```javascript
// Using Playwright
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('complete onboarding flow', async ({ page }) => {
  // Seed deterministic test data
  await seedTestUser({ email: 'demo@test.com', password: 'Test123!' });

  // Mock email service to capture verification link
  await mockEmailService();

  // Mock OIDC provider
  await mockOIDCProvider();

  // Navigate to signup and complete registration
  await page.goto('/signup');
  await page.fill('[name=email]', 'demo@test.com');
  await page.fill('[name=password]', 'Test123!');
  await page.click('[data-testid=signup-submit]');

  // Extract and follow email verification link
  const verifyLink = await getVerificationLink('demo@test.com');
  await page.goto(verifyLink);

  // Complete profile setup
  await page.fill('[name=name]', 'Jane Doe');
  await page.fill('[name=company]', 'Acme Corp');
  await page.click('[data-testid=profile-submit]');

  // Create first workspace
  await page.fill('[name=workspaceName]', 'My Workspace');
  await page.click('[data-testid=create-workspace]');

  // Assert dashboard displays new workspace
  await expect(page.locator('[data-testid=workspace-card]')).toContainText('My Workspace');
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests at the beginner level often miss realistic offline scenarios and deterministic retries. This question probes practical E2E thinking: isolation, network control, and idempotent submission.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end orchestration of multi-step flows
- Auto-save timing and state persistence (e.g., localStorage/IndexedDB)
- Offline/online transition handling and reconciling state
- Mocking external APIs (identity) for determinism
- Ensuring single, idempotent final submit

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (context-level offline, data isolation, mocked API)
const context = await browser.newContext({ storageState: 'test-user.json' });
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.setOffline(true);
await page.goto('https://finapp/onboarding');
// perform step interactions, wait for auto-save marker, then go online
